-- title:
--   One more puncture adds one more free generator
-- statement:
--   The inductive step in the computation of the fundamental group of a punctured plane: adding one more puncture adds one more free generator.
--
--   Precisely, if $\pi_1(E^2 - Q_n)$ is free of rank $n$, then $\pi_1(E^2 - Q_{n+1})$ is free of rank $n+1$.
--
--   This is the one remaining ingredient. The base case is elementary and is proved in the parent submission: with no punctures the space is the whole plane, which is contractible, so both sides are trivial. Everything else about the statement for general $n$ follows by induction from the step asserted here.
--
--   The classical proof is an application of the Seifert--van Kampen theorem. Cover $E^2 - Q_{n+1}$ by two open sets: a neighbourhood $U$ of the new puncture, homeomorphic to a punctured disc and so homotopy equivalent to a circle, and the complement $V$ of that puncture's closed disc together with the rest of the plane, which deformation retracts onto $E^2 - Q_n$. Their intersection is an annulus, which is connected and has free cyclic fundamental group generated by a loop that is null-homotopic in $V$. Van Kampen then identifies $\pi_1$ of the union with the free product $\pi_1(V) * \pi_1(U) \cong F_n * \mathbb{Z} \cong F_{n+1}$.
--
--   Two remarks on what makes this the right place to cut.
--
--   The step is genuinely a *gluing* statement, not a covering-space one. That distinction matters because covering-space computations of fundamental groups are available --- Mathlib has the deck-transformation classification for quotient covering maps, from which for instance the fundamental group of the circle is a short instantiation. Gluing has no counterpart: there is no Seifert--van Kampen theorem in the library, nor any pushout or colimit construction for fundamental groupoids. So this single step is exactly the boundary between what is reachable and what is not.
--
--   The $\mathbb{Z}$ factor contributed by the new puncture is the fundamental group of a circle, so a proof may reasonably cite `AlgebraicTopology.fundamentalGroup_circle_equiv_int`, which is proved. What that result does not supply is the amalgamation: knowing each piece does not by itself determine the union.
-- source:
--   Classical; Hatcher, Algebraic Topology, Example 1.21 (wedge of circles) and Theorem 1.20 (Seifert--van Kampen). Cf. Birman, Braids, Links and Mapping Class Groups, Chapter 1.

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_BraidsLinksMCG_ConfigSpace

namespace BraidsLinksMCG

theorem puncturedPlaneGroup_succ_step (n : ℕ)
    (h : Nonempty (PuncturedPlaneGroup n ≃* FreeGroup (Fin n))) :
    Nonempty (PuncturedPlaneGroup (n + 1) ≃* FreeGroup (Fin (n + 1))) := by sorry

end BraidsLinksMCG
